  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 213 PM MST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA decent consensus of models and guidance sources lean away from \nanother round of fog and low stratus development tonight into \nSunday morning despite RH values forecast to again reach 90-100 \npercent across the region (not to mention continuity from recent \ndays which have featured fog and stratus issues). That seems a bit\n\"juicy\" to leave patchy fog out of the forecast completely amid an\nonly slightly drier overall environment/no significant airmass\nchange. Granted, the lower atmosphere across especially our South\nDakota plains may remain a bit more mixed/less stable tonight \nwith slightly breezy conditions in place, but winds should remain\nmore calm across our northwestern South Dakota plains as well as \njust west of the Black Hills, and these areas are exactly where \nwe do see hints of fog/low stratus potential reflected in NBM and\nHRRR guidance. Thus, have added a mention of just patchy fog to \nthese areas along with some limited cloud cover. We'll have to \nmonitor trends to see if other portions of the CWA become more \nfavored for development as well. Burn-off of anything that does\ndevelop will likely be more progressive Sunday morning compared \nto today.\n\nOtherwise, the forecast over the next few days leans mild, dry,\nand continued slightly breezy for some areas as the central US\nremains mostly dominated by longwave ridging, with longwave\ntroughing residing along the Pacific coast. Several shortwave\npieces of energy attempt to break off/eject inland over the \ncourse of the next week. The first of any consequence comes \nthrough Tuesday night and Wednesday with some potential for rain \nand snow showers, but local QPF amounts currently look quite light\nwith this. Our temperatures will take a hit though, with highs in\nthe 50s/60s Tuesday only reaching the 40s/50s starting Wednesday.\nA much more significant inland-ejection of the coastal trough \ncomes Friday into next weekend with a better chance of more \nwidespread precipitation (and a continued cooling of \ntemperatures...think increasing chance of snow), although forecast\nconfidence in QPF details remains low at this early juncture. \nEarly WPC and model- blend QPF projections are currently jumping \naround a bit. This feature could weaken as it crosses the Rockies,\nwith 500mb height cluster analysis EOF patterns pointing at \nstrength/amplitude being the primary driver of forecast \nuncertainty at this time (compared to exact timing for our area, \nalthough that could eventually change too). All-in, if you're \nlooking for warmer temperatures and low-impact weather for \nanything outdoors, today through about Tuesday will be your best \nwindow, with Wednesday and Thursday potentially okay too but \ncooler.",
